  "investigation_notes": "Diagnosis: **insufficient_evidence**\n\nWhat is known:\n- App container `incidentlab-app-1` is `running`, `Up 5 seconds` (E1), confirmed `running: true` with `started_at 2026-10-29T15:54:30Z` (E5).\n- DB container `incidentlab-db-1` is `running`, `Up 11 seconds (healthy)` (E1), confirmed `running: true` with `health: healthy` (E6).\n- App `/health` returns `503 {\"status\":\"unhealthy\"}` (E2), so uploads failing is consistent with an unhealthy app, but this is a symptom not a root cause.\n- DB dependency shows repeated `pg_isready` checks with `exit_code 0` and `health_status: healthy` (E7), and `oom_killed: false` for both containers is not present - specifically app shows `oom_killed: false` (E5) and db shows `oom_killed: false` (E6).\n- App mounts `source /srv/lab-data -> destination /data, rw: true` as `user 1000:1000` (E5). DB mounts volume to `/var/lib/postgresql/data` (E6). Both on `incidentlab_default` network (E5, E6).\n- Recent Docker events show `destroy/rename/start` and `kill/stop/die/create` recreations at `15:53:43` and `15:54:24` with app `start` after db `healthy` (E7).\n- Filesystem holding uploads: `/srv/lab-data` shows `use_percent 9.0` and `inode_use_percent 0.2` with `available_mb 410.6` (E8). Root filesystem shows `use_percent 6.8` and `inode_use_percent 1.5` (E9), ruling out capacity/inode exhaustion as observed.\n- Direct evidence for upload writes and app errors missing: app logs unavailable (E3), db logs unavailable (E4), permission/writability checks for `/srv/lab-data/uploads` unavailable (E10, E12) and for `/srv/lab-data` unavailable (E11).\n\nWhy insufficient_evidence:\n- DB healthy (E6, E7) and disk not full (E8, E9) and no OOM (E5) rule out those common causes, but per instructions a cause cannot be concluded by elimination alone.\n- The critical check for this symptom - whether `user 1000:1000` (E5) can write to the bind-mounted upload directory (E5) - could not be obtained because path_access was unavailable (E10, E11, E12).\n- App internal error could not be obtained because logs were unavailable (E3, E4).\n- Therefore the root cause itself was not observed. Repairs should not be approved on current evidence; retry logs and path_access collection.\n",
